is. Has your research influenced your own tipping behavior? I mean, do you think about research when you're eating out and the check comes? Yes, and it's influenced it in a couple of ways. First off, because I do a lot of interviews like this and people ask me about tipping, my own tipping habits, that puts pressure on me to be able to truthfully say I'm a good tipper. And so I routinely give 18% in restaurants because I don't, I don't. I don't want to seem like I'm extravagant, but I don't want to be cheap. But having said that, my awareness of actual tipping behavior in many of these new context in that people are asking for tips, but consumers aren't giving them in many of these

